variants.reconstruction:
%n šar (|) gimir (|) dadmē | bānû (|) kibrā[ti (|) ...]
#note: The interpretation of the opening lines of the poem is a @i{vexata quaestio}. The most serious difficulty is the identification of the character described as “king of all settlements,” who could conceivably be Išum, Erra, or even Marduk (see already @bib{RN31@135-137}). It seems easiest to interpret that the poem begins with a hymn to Išum, as defended by @bib{mueller1995wer@349-360}, @bib{RN1593@48-49}, and @bib{RN455@60-61}, among others. @bib{RN31@138} assumed that the missing portion of the line contained “un terzo epiteto divino, composto di due o tre elementi, che dia al verso lo stesso ritmo ternario dei vv. 3-5.” @bib{RN675@263}, who is of the opinion that the opening lines are addressed to Erra, restores [@i{qurādu Erra}], “[warrior Erra].”
// L 0.0 NA "-" b+1

%n attā | dipārum-ma || inaṭṭalū | nūrka
#note: Compare @bib{RN3058@4a}: “Earlier in the poem of Erra and Išum, Erra’s heart explicitly identifies Išum as @i{diparu}, “a firebrand,” that the gods follow into battle (I 10). From this it follows that the light which the night watchmen Išum and Ḫendursanga shone to lead people home at night was a burning torch or firebrand. Indeed, it seems likely that Išum’s divine power was instantiated in the fiery glow of the torch, and that his name was indeed the masculine form of the Akkadian common noun @i{išātum}, “fire.””
// cf. L III.4 38

%n ša sebetti | qarrād (|) lā šanān || šunnât | ilūssun
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 3
// cf. L I.2 SB "I" 91
// cf. F K.128 13

%n ītammi | ana šalši | zīm (|) labbi | lū šaknātā-ma | āmirka | lišharmiṭ
// cf. L I.5 SB "III" 124
// cf. L I.5 SB "IV" 21
// cf. L I.2 SB "I" 139
// cf. L I.2 SB "II" 25
// cf. L I.2 SB "III" 2
// cf. L I.2 SB "III" 128

%n šunu | ezzū-ma || tebû | kakkūšun
#note: On the expression @i{tebû kakkūšun}, “their weapons are raised,” see @bib{RN117@785}.
// cf. L I.4 SB "I" 63
// cf. L I.4 SB "I" 80

%n ana ālik (|) ṣēri | akî || itarraṣ | qāssu
#note: @i{akî} here and in l. 56 has been universally interpreted as an interrogative adverb @i{akī}, which makes the understanding of these lines difficult. As noted in the comment to III 54, it is best to interpret it as the adjective @i{akû}, “humble.” 
// cf. L I.4 SB "III" 61

%n hursānū | zaqrūtu | lišmû-ma | l[ītabb]itū-ma? | lišpilā | rēš[āš]un
#note: On the restoration @akk{l[i-tab-b]i-tu-ma}, see @bib{RN31@168}, where also an alternative @akk{[liš-tal-pi]-tu-ma}, “let them be leveled,” is suggested.
// cf. L I.5 SB "I" 35

%n būlu | līrur-ma || litūr | ana ṭiṭṭi
#note: See the commentary on the parallel passage in IV 150.
// L I.5 SB "IV" 150

%n amāt (|) se[bet]ti | iqbû | kī ulu (|) š[amn]i | elīšu | iṭīb
// cf. L I.4 SB "II" 69
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 129

%n ṭūda | pitē-ma || luṣbat | harrā[n]a
// L I.5 SB "II" 128
// L I.5 SB "III" 130

%n sebettu | qarrād (|) lā šanān || lupp[išū] | t[āhāz]a
#note: The parallel in III 133 allows now the correct restoration of the line. @bib{RN31@176} reconstructed @akk{lu-up#-p[it?]}.
// L I.5 SB "III" 131

%n u attā | ālik (|) mahrīya || alik | pā[nī]ya
#note: Formerly @i{arkīya} was reconstructed at the end, apparently confirmed by collation of MS AššNA2 (so @bib{RN31@177 fn. 116}). However, as already suggested by Borger @i{apud} @bib{RN31@177 fn. 116}, the obvious command to a “champion” (@i{ālik maḫrīya}) would be for him to “march ahead” (@i{alik pānīya}), a supposition now confirmed by the identical line in III 133.
// L I.5 SB "III" 133

%n ana sapān | mātāti | hulluq (|) [nišīšin | lemu]tta | takpud-ma | lā t[atūr | ana a]rkīka
#note: Compare the close paralllel in an Esarhaddon inscription mentioned by @bib{RN31@178}: @akk{{d}EN.LIL₂.LA₅ | DINGIR#-MEŠ {d}AMAR.UTU a-na sa-pan | KUR# hul-lu-qu UN-MEŠ-ša₂ | ik-ta-pu-ud HUL-ti₃}, “The Enlil of [the go]ds, the god Marduk, became angry and plotted evilly to level the land (and) to destroy its people” (@bib{RN393@Esarhaddon 104} i 34-37).
// L I.5 SB "II" 27
// L I.5 SB "II" 67
// L I.5 SB "III" 142
// L I.5 SB "V" 40

%n īšum | qūlam-ma || šime | qabâya
// L I.5 SB "III" 180
// L I.5 SB "V" 17

%n ālik (|) mahri (|) ilī | enqu | īšum | ša milikšu | damqu
#note: As noted by @bib{RN1811@43}, @bib{RN55aa@148}, and @bib{RN31@178}, the passage is ironic. Gössmann speaks of “offensichtlich beißende Ironie.”
// L I.5 SB "III" 182

%n ina šamê | rīmāku || ina erṣeti | labbāku
#note: @bib{RN1811@43} calls this passage “ein Musterbeispiel bab. Poesie.” See the thorough study of the lines in @bib{cohen2013fearful@1-28}.
// L I.5 SB "III" 183
// L III.7 54

%n ina būli | māhiṣāku || ina šadî | šubāku
#note: On the translation “archer” or “bowhunter” of @i{māḫiṣu}, and “pit-trap” of @i{šubāku}, see the commentary on III 186.
// L I.5 SB "III" 186
// cf. L I.5 SB "IV" 93

%n ana n[a]mî | errub-ma | nergal | ram[âk]u | šubta
#note: Previous attempts at restoring this line (e.g. @bib{RN31@180 fn. 125}, @bib{cagni1977poem@31 n. 30}, @bib{NABU2013-10@10}, @bib{cohen2013fearful@4-5}) can now be proven wrong thanks to the parallel in tablet III.
// L I.5 SB "III" 192

%n ilū | napharšunu || nib[ī]tī | šahtū
#note: Previous attempts at restoring this line (e.g. @bib{RN31@180}) should now be abandoned in view of the parallel in tablet III. It is interpreted that the writing @i{-ta} in @akk{ni-b[i!?]-ta#} reflects an implicit first-person suffix @i{-i}, since the parallel in III 193 does have the suffix.
// L I.5 SB "III" 193

%n u nišī | ṣalmā[t (|) q]aqqadi || leq[û] | šēṭūtī
#note: @bib{RN31@181} notes that this is the only instance in which @i{šēṭūtu} is used without a personal suffix, and speculates that the suffix may have been omitted “per indicare un atteggiamento più generale.” Note, however, that the suffix is written in the parallel line III 197, and that MS HuzNA1 also omits the first-person possessive suffix of a word in the previous line.
// L I.5 SB "III" 194
// cf. L I.5 SB "III" 212
// cf. L I.5 SB "IV" 113

%n u ša rubê | marūtuk | amāssu | iddû-ma | eppuš(u) | kī libbuš
#note: The understanding of @akk{ep-pu-šu} // @akk{[e]p-pu-uš} in the first person follows a suggestion by E. Tadmor.
// L I.5 SB "III" 196

%n qurādu | erra | aššu šipri | šâšu | ša taqbû | epēša
#note: According to @bib{RN164@887}, the line refers to I 123.
// cf. L I.5 SB "I" 149

%n ina šub[t]īya | atbē-ma | šipīt (|) šamê (|) (u) erṣeti | uptaṭṭir
#note: @akk{ši-BIT} is interpreted as @i{šipītu}, “seam” (following Landsberger’s proposal @i{apud} @bib{aro1958bemerkungen@25}, recommended also by @bib{RN1445@26}; on this word, see @bib{RN3043@225}, who advocates for a transcription with /@i{p}/). Compare the interpretation as @i{šipṭu}, “equilibrium,” in @bib{RN706@399b} and @bib{RN3209@155-156}. Compare also the understanding “Strafgericht Urteil” in @bib{hruška1974letzten@357 fn. 22}.
// cf. L I.5 SB "I" 170
// cf. L I.5 SB "II" 1

%n šamê | ša uštarʾibū | ša kakkabī (|) šamāmī | manzāssunu | išnī-ma | ul utīr | ašruššun
#note: Compare @bib{RN706@401a}: “Each of these difficult lines presents the same syntactic pattern: a relative clause without an antecedent acts as a casus pendens: «With reference to the quaking of the heavens(!) …, with reference to the shaking of the underworld …, with reference to the disturbing of the equilibrium of the universe …».”
// cf. L I.5 SB "IV" 123

%n erkallu | ša inūšu | ša šerʾi | bilassu | imṭī-ma | adi ulla | ana emēdi | ašṭa
#note: Compare the interpretation in @bib{RN2721@A/2 476}: “(the yield of the furrow became so little that) it was difficult to levy taxes (on it)” (followed also by @bib{RN31@187}).
// cf. L I.5 SB "IV" 123

%n kakk[īy]a | ušatbā-ma || uhall[a]q | rēha
#note: On the substantivized adjective @i{rēḫu}, “rest,” see @bib{RN358@35}, who understands the phrase in this context as “auch noch den Rest vernichten.” @bib{RN31@189-190}, following a suggestion by Deller, interprets it rather as a stative 3f plural, understanding @i{nišū} as the subject: “(Sebbene?) io alzi le mie armi e distrugga — sopravvivono (ancora).” The formulation of AššNA2, taken here in the main text, is understood here as an irrealis (on irrealis in sentences connected with @i{-ma}, see @bib{krebernik2001šumman@73-74}.
// cf. L I.5 SB "V" 41
// cf. L III.10 54

%n ummânī | šunūti | ana apsî | ušērid-ma || elâšunu | ul aqbi
#note: The passage is discussed in length by @bib{RN31@190-192}. According to him, the passage refers to the seven @i{apkallū} of the Apsû.
// cf. L I.5 SB "II" 29

%n ša ina tâmti | rapašti | (ina) mê | meʾat (|) bērī | išissu | ikšudu | šupul (|) aralli
#note: The @i{mēsu}-tree appears here as @i{axis mundi}, as a cosmic tree spanning heaven and earth (see @bib{RN31@192}, @bib{RN220@28}, @bib{stol2017review@376}). Compare the famous description of the passage in @bib{RN949@198} (also cited by @bib{RN31@193}): “Wenn sich im Erra-Epos der senile Marduk darüber beschwert, daß er nach der Sintflut die vorsintflutlichen (im wahren Sinne des Wortes!) @i{mēšu}-Bäume, von denen das @i{e}[@i{lmēšu}] kommt (das Wortspiel @i{mēšu} : @i{elmešu} ist zu beachten!), nicht wieder auffinden konnte, so wird sicher niemand Bekanntschaft mit den antediluvialen Bernsteinwäldern voraussetzen; wohl aber die halbmythische Vorstellung, daß Bernstein ein Baumharz sei (Plinius zitiert bei Meyer, @i{Konv. Lex}.⁵ II 866) bzw. eine Variante der Fabel, daß „Bernstein von Nußbäumen“ stamme (Löw 2, 61 Anm. 1).”
// cf. L I.5 SB "IV" 49

%n ali | ebbu | zagindurû || ša ušamsakū | uqnâ
#note: Lambert @i{apud} @bib{RN31@194-195} suggests parsing the verb as @i{nasāqu}, “to choose.” Here the interpretation as @i{nasāku}, “in Ordnung bringen o.ä.” (so @bib{borger1971tempelbau@79-80}) is preferred. Although the verb could be a first person, it seems preferable to interpret it as a third person plural, either as an impersonal form, or with the gods that appear in the following lines as its subject. On @i{zagindurû} as “Egyptian blue,” see @bib{thavapalan2020meaning@355-366}, with previous bibliography. @i{uqnû}, now restored thanks to MS TarNA1, is interpreted as a predicative complement (on this category, see @bib{fromebllab0003@79-81}); compare II 34: @i{šumsukat eli ša mahri}, “I made more splendid than before.”
// cf. L I.5 SB "II" 32

%n [ina š]ubtīya | atebbī-ma || šipīt šamê (|) erṣeti | uptaṭṭar
// cf. L I.5 SB "I" 133
// cf. L I.5 SB "II" 1

%n [šāru] | lemnu | iziqqam-ma | ša nišī | šiknāt (|) napišt[i] | niṭi[lšin | uṭṭa?]
#note: The restoration adopted here was first proposed by @bib{temp_id_07390035038145881@208}, and followed also by @bib{RN31@199}.
// cf. L I.5 SB "I" 187

%n [ša] qablī (|) paṭrāti | māhiršun[u | ...]
#note: On the expression @i{qabla paṭāru}, “to undress,” see @bib{RN117@873 ad 181}.
// cf. F K.1109 o 29

%n rubû | marūtuk | adi attā | ana bīti | šâšu | terrubū-ma | gīru | ṣubātka | ubbabū-ma | tatūra | ašrukka
#note: “That house” refers to the place in which Marduk’s statue will be refurbished, i.e. the @i{bīt mummi} or workshop.
// cf. L I.5 SB "I" 141
